// Disaster: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== disaster.f01 | feature | real-time
title: All Saints
genre: disaster + historical-drama
tone: Bright, sudden and merciless; then very still
era: 1755 | period
setting: Lisbon, the morning of All Saints' Day | real | city
logline: On the morning an earthquake, a wave and a fire destroy Lisbon in turn, a customs clerk who alone knows the river is about to return must turn a praying crowd away from the open quay.
premise: It is a feast day; every candle in the city is lit. At twenty to ten the ground moves for six minutes. The survivors run from the falling streets to the widest open space they know, the new marble quay on the Tagus, and kneel. {joao}, a clerk in the customs house, has read a sea captain's account of Callao. He watches the river draw back from the walls.
synopsis: The film runs from the first bell to nightfall. {joao} is late for mass, quarrelling with his sister {ines}, a novice, about their mother's house. The first shock throws the customs ledgers on him and that saves him. In the street the churches have come down on the people inside them. He finds {ines} digging at the convent with her hands. They go where everyone goes: downhill, to the quay. A Jesuit, {teles}, is leading the crowd in penitence; it is God's judgement, and the open sky is safe. Then the harbour empties. The riverbed lies bare, with wrecks and cargo in the mud, and people walk out to look. {joao} knows what a sea does when it goes away. He has perhaps a quarter of an hour, no authority, and a priest who says that to run is to doubt. He cannot move the crowd by argument. {ines} can: she takes off her veil, picks up a stranger's child and walks uphill, and women follow a nun where they would not follow a clerk. Some hundreds go. The wave takes the quay and everyone on it. By afternoon the candles have done the rest and the city is burning from the river to the castle. On the hill at dusk, {amaro}, an English wine factor who has lost his warehouse and found his neighbour's children, asks {joao} what is to be done. {joao} gives the answer a minister will make famous tomorrow: bury the dead and feed the living. Then he starts writing down the names of who is on the hill, because he is a clerk, and somebody must.
question: When the ground itself fails, whom do people follow: the one who explains it, or the one who moves?
themes: knowledge without authority; faith and flight; the first act of rebuilding is a list
audience: Adults and older teenagers
viewpoint: With {joao}, never more than a street's width from him
stakes: The thousands kneeling on the quay; a brother and sister's last chance to stop arguing
conflict: A clerk who has read one book against a city that has decided the open water is where God will spare it
ending: The quay is gone. Those who walked uphill behind a novice are alive. The city burns. {joao} writes names.
experience: Six minutes of terror, a quarter hour of unbearable suspense, and a strange calm in the ashes
look: Blue-and-white tile, gold altars, then dust; the drained riverbed shining; fire seen from a hill
sound: Bells that ring themselves as towers sway. Then no bells at all.
m: protect | nature | faith-vs-evidence | pyrrhic
@ joao | Joao Mascarenhas | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 29 | To get people off the quay | He has read what happened at Callao | He is nobody, and a priest disagrees | Bookish, argumentative, physically timid | From knowing to acting to recording | Fast, precise, apologises mid-sentence | Ink-stained cuffs, one shoe lost by reel two
@ ines | Sister Ines Mascarenhas | co-lead | deuteragonist | human | she/her | 24 | To do what is asked of her today | She took the veil to escape a family quarrel | Her vow of obedience, and a Jesuit giving orders | Stubborn, practical, more worldly than her brother | From obedience to leading | Plain, warm, will not raise her voice | A novice's white veil, then no veil
@ teles | Father Gaspar Teles | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 66 | To save souls in the hour given | He believes this is judgement | He is sincere | Charismatic, fearless, immovable | Unchanged; he stays on the quay | Thunder, Italian-accented Portuguese | Black soutane grey with dust
@ amaro | Thomas Amory | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 41 | His warehouse, then anyone at all | He is a Protestant in a city that blames them | He has two children who are not his by the hand | Brisk, commercial, unexpectedly brave | From saving stock to saving strangers | English merchant's Portuguese, counts everything | Wine-stained coat, a ledger under his arm
~ joao - ines | brother and sister | a quarrel over a house | a hill, and a list they write together
~ joao - teles | clerk and preacher | deference | a shouted argument at the water's edge
~ ines - teles | novice and Jesuit | obedience | she turns her back and walks
